{
 "about": [
  "REQ-DEV-041 regex dialect cases (asancart documents api/device-api.md: T2.15, T3.55, T2.16, T3.56, T2.17, T2.18, T2.19, T2.23, T2.32, T2.34, T2.58, T2.59), the shared case set every engine runs. Not SMS data.",
  "allowed: inside the dialect; each text is matched after REQ-DEV-050's always-on steps, as a full match: the text matches ^(?:P)$ (REQ-DEV-041); groups lists named groups for some texts, each of which must match (T4.24; null: did not take part).",
  "flag i folds ASCII letters only (T2.18): every engine rewrites the pattern (k to [kK], [a-f] to [a-fA-F]) and compiles it without its own case-insensitive option.",
  "refused: outside the dialect; tokens are the code dialect details, one per distinct token, in order of first occurrence.",
  "empty-match rule (T2.19, T2.32): a group of any kind that can match the empty string takes only {1}, {1,1}, {0} or {0,0}, lazy or not, bounds read as numbers (T2.34); any other quantifier is refused, the quantifier as written as token. Lookahead rule (T2.23): a named group inside (?= or (?! is refused, its opener as token.",
  "exactly-once rule (T2.58): a named group at any depth inside a group quantified {1} or {1,1} (numeric value, lazy or not) is refused, its opener as token, as inside a group that can repeat (T3.56). A named group itself quantified {1} stays allowed.",
  "bound rule (T2.59): a quantifier {n}, {n,} or {n,m} with n or m above 1000, read as a number without overflow (leading zeros dropped), n > m included, is refused on anything, the quantifier as written as token (a lazy ? included; a possessive one as possessive only).",
  "regex: inside the dialect, but no engine compiles it (code regex).",
  "Run on the JVM by :core:test, on Android's ICU by the app's instrumented tests, and against JavaScript RegExp (the admin engine's library) by scripts/check-dialect-cases.mjs."
 ],
